---
title: Muscle group insight
---

A muscle group insight algorithm looks at your training history for one muscle
group — its weekly volume and the trend of every exercise that primarily
targets it — and says what (if anything) your own data suggests about the
volume/frequency that works for you. The user picks one in
**Settings → Muscle groups**; the app calls it once per muscle group whenever
that tab is viewed.

Unlike a [progression algorithm](/docs/plugins/progression), the input here
isn't one exercise's own history — a muscle group's volume is inherently
cross-exercise. The app owns fetching every session and resolving muscle tags
into the series below; your algorithm only sees the resolved data and decides
what it means.

## The contract

```ts
export const pluginBundle = {
  formatVersion: 1,
  pluginId: "com.you.my-insight",
  family: "muscle-group-insight",
  entryExport: "algorithm",
} as const;

export const algorithm = {
  id: "com.you.my-insight",
  name: "My Insight",
  description: "Static RP-style volume landmarks instead of a learned correlation.",
  defaultState: null,

  analyze(input) {
    const totalSets = input.weeks.reduce((sum, w) => sum + w.weightedSets, 0);
    const avgWeeklySets = input.weeks.length ? totalSets / input.weeks.length : 0;

    return {
      nextState: input.state,
      reasoning: {
        inputs: { weeksWithData: input.weeks.length, avgWeeklySets: Math.round(avgWeeklySets) },
        computed: {},
        confidence: input.weeks.length >= 4 ? "medium" : "low",
        verdict:
          avgWeeklySets < 10
            ? "below typical maintenance volume for this muscle group"
            : "within typical training range",
      },
    };
  },
};
```

## Input — `MuscleGroupInsightInput`

See `@logit/core/domain/muscleGroupInsight`.

| Field | Notes |
|---|---|
| `muscleGroup` | Which muscle group this call is for. |
| `weeks` | `{ weekBucket, weightedSets, sessionCount }[]`, oldest → newest. `weekBucket` is an opaque, comparable/orderable bucket id — match it against a series point's `date` via `weekBucket()` from `@logit/core/domain/time`, don't convert it back to a timestamp yourself. |
| `contributingExercises` | Only exercises where this muscle is a **primary** tag — each with its `status` (the same `ProgressStatus` as `/progress`) and oldest→newest `seriesPoints` (`{ date, value }`, its primary-metric series). |
| `state` | Your opaque value — whatever you returned as `nextState` last time. |
| `userPreferences` | Values the user set, if you declare a `preferencesSchema`. |
| `now` | Epoch ms, passed in so the algorithm stays pure. |

## Output — `MuscleGroupInsightOutput`

```ts
type MuscleGroupInsightOutput = {
  volumeInsight?: {
    direction: "higher" | "lower";   // which side of thresholdSets correlates with more improved sessions
    thresholdSets: number;
    improveRateAbove: number;        // 0-1
    improveRateBelow: number;        // 0-1
  };
  nextState: unknown;                // persisted by the app, handed back next call
  reasoning: Reasoning;              // required here, unlike ProgressionOutput — see below
};
```

`reasoning` (`@logit/core/domain/reasoning`) isn't optional for this family: it's
how the "not enough data yet" state gets communicated honestly instead of a
silent or overconfident verdict. `volumeInsight` is the only opinionated part —
omit it whenever your algorithm doesn't have enough support for a claim yet, and
say so via `reasoning.verdict`/`confidence` instead of guessing.

## The built-in algorithm

`learned-muscle-group-insight` (id `learned-muscle-group-insight`) is the
learned option, not a fixed formula: it stays silent until there are at least 8
weeks of data, then does a median-split correlation between weekly volume and
session-over-session improvement across `contributingExercises`. A plugin
implementing this same contract can swap in a static table (RP-style
MEV/MAV/MRV numbers, or anything else) behind the identical interface — the
settings picker treats both the same way.

## Preferences, build & test

Identical to [progression algorithms](/docs/plugins/progression): export a
`preferencesSchema`, bundle with esbuild, test `analyze` as a pure function,
then [publish](/docs/plugins/publishing).
